--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
C -- ASYMPTOTIC SATURATION.  Delta' > 0 at every finite s but Delta' -> 0: is the LIMIT divergence harmful?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  if Delta'(s) >= h > 0 for all s then Delta(S) - Delta(0) >= h S -> oo: Delta is unbounded.
  hence bounded + increasing => inf_s Delta'(s) = 0 => sup_s Sigma_par(s) = +infinity, unavoidably.
  [FAIL] C1 bounded + everywhere-increasing does NOT force Delta' -> 0 (i.e. an admissible kernel can keep Sigma_par uniformly bounded)   (Delta' >= h > 0 integrates to an unbounded Delta.  The stiffness divergence in the LIMIT is unavoidable for every member of the class; only its harmfulness is in question)
  L2 coefficient  Sigma_par = W' + 2 g^2 W''            (checked as a polynomial identity)
  L3 coefficient  kappa3    = 2 g W'' + (4/3) g^3 W'''
  identity        kappa3    = (1/3) d Sigma_par / d g_phi
  [PASS] C2 [control] sympy: the cubic coefficient of the action expansion is EXACTLY (1/3) dSigma_par/dg_phi, so the strong-coupling amplitude is g_* = 3 |d ln Sigma_par / d g_phi|^{-1}   (kappa3 - (1/3) dSigma/dg = 0)
  g_*(s) = 3 Sigma_par / |dSigma_par/dg_phi| = 3 a0 Delta'(s)^2 / |Delta''(s)|
  [PASS] C3 [control] sympy: g_*(s) = 3 a0 Delta'(s)^2 / |Delta''(s)|, exactly, for an arbitrary Delta   (residual 0)

